  "other_facts": "Background: Worldwide obesity is a public health challenge with increased risk of insulin resistance and type 2 diabetes. Costa Rica lacks epidemiological data on pediatric type 2 diabetes prevalence, but the Children's National Hospital Endocrinology Department noted increased cases in the last 2 to 3 years. Methods: Anthropometric measurements and blood determinations of glucose, insulin, proinsulin, glycosylated hemoglobin, C-peptide, and leptin were performed. Indexes were calculated to assess insulin resistance. Information on social and lifestyle variables was obtained from questionnaires, and acanthosis nigricans was certified by a physician. Statistical analysis was performed with SPSS software for Windows, version 10.0. Results: Girls and obese children (BMI >= 95th percentile) were more likely to have higher serum insulin levels and insulin resistance than boys and overweight children (BMI >= 85th percentile). Children in the highest quintile of body-fat tissue had higher insulin resistance but similar serum concentrations of glucose, C-peptide, and proinsulin compared to the lowest quintile. Conclusions: Strategies for weight reduction, obesity prevention, and promotion of healthy lifestyles are necessary to prevent the onset of type 2 diabetes during childhood and adolescence.",
